NAME
       wc - count lines, words and bytes

SYNOPSIS
       wc [-l] [-w] [-c] [-m] [-L] [FILE]...

DESCRIPTION
       wc prints, for each FILE (or standard input), the number of lines,
       words and bytes, followed by the file name. With several files a
       total line is added. A word is a run of characters between blanks.
       With options only the chosen counts are printed, always in the order
       lines, words, characters, bytes.

OPTIONS
       -l, --lines
              Count lines (newline characters).

       -w, --words
              Count words.

       -c, --bytes
              Count bytes.

       -m, --chars
              Count characters (differs from bytes for non-ASCII text).

       -L, --max-line-length
              Print the length of the longest line.

EXAMPLES
       How many accounts are there?

           wc -l /etc/passwd

       How many lines contain "error" in the log? (grep -c does the same)

           grep -i error /var/log/messages | wc -l

       Print only the number, without the file name:

           wc -l < /root/found.txt

SEE ALSO
       grep(1), sort(1), uniq(1)
